course: dictionary
lesson: 1318
title: Calcium in context
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. «Calcium, found in milk and cheese, builds strong bones and teeth.» Here calcium is a mineral your body needs. The words builds, bones and teeth are the clues.
explain: Read the science sentences carefully. «Eggshell and chalk are calcium carbonate, so they fizz in vinegar too.» Now calcium is part of a substance that makes rocks, chalk and shells. Ask yourself: is this sentence about the body, or about rocks and metals?
explain: Try the swap check. Put your meaning into the sentence: «Bones need calcium, from foods like milk, cheese and green vegetables, to stay strong.» If the sentence still makes sense with "a mineral that bones need", you have found the right meaning.
fact: Calcium is a mineral that our bodies need for strong bones and teeth.
fact: Calcium is also a chemical element, a soft silver metal found in rocks, chalk, shells and milk.
fact: Foods that give calcium include milk, cheese, yoghurt, green vegetables, beans and tofu.
fact: Vitamin D helps the body absorb calcium.
fact: «Calcium carbonate» is the substance in eggshell, chalk and limestone, and it fizzes in vinegar.
exercise: «Bones need calcium, from foods like milk, cheese and green vegetables, to stay strong.» What does calcium help bones do? | Bend and stretch | Turn into milk | *Stay strong | Glow in the dark
exercise: «Calcium, found in milk and cheese, builds strong bones and teeth.» Where is calcium found? | Only at the bottom of the sea | *In milk and cheese | In gold coins | In the sky
exercise: «Eggshell and chalk are calcium carbonate, so they fizz in vinegar too.» What is chalk made of? | Plastic | Wood pulp | *Calcium carbonate | Sugar and water
test: Calcium is described as a soft silver metal. Where is it found? | Only in the sky | Only in cars | *In rocks, chalk, shells and milk | In plastic bags
test: The sentence says vitamin D helps the body absorb calcium. What does absorb mean here? | Hide in the bones | Throw away | *Take in and use | Turn into sugar
exam: Which of these is named as a food that gives calcium? | Chips | Sugar sweets | *Yoghurt | Crisps
